       "content": "# Scope and Limitations\n\n## Scope\n\nThis study investigates and defines the effectiveness of a precision drilling alignment tool in promoting accurate and consistent drilling centers across multiple repeated trials. The research is bounded by the following parameters:\n\n**1. Primary Focus on Alignment Guides**\nThe study examines the functionality of the tool's alignment guides as the principal mechanism for centering and positioning. This includes evaluating how the guides interact with the workpiece surface, their repeatability under successive engagements, and their contribution to minimizing lateral and angular deviation from the intended drill center.\n\n**2. Repeated-Trial Consistency**\nThe investigation is centered on the tool's performance over a defined number of repeated drilling trials (e.g., 20\u201350 cycles per configuration). The study measures the statistical consistency of center placement\u2014expressed through metrics such as mean deviation, standard deviation, and coefficient of variation\u2014rather than a single-instance accuracy assessment.\n\n**3. Range of Workpiece Dimensions**\nThe tool's precision is assessed across a specified range of workpiece geometries, including variations in thickness, diameter (for cylindrical stock), and surface area. The dimensional boundaries are defined by the tool's stated operational envelope (e.g., minimum and maximum workpiece thickness, minimum hole diameter, and maximum plate width).\n\n**4. Material Variability**\nThe study evaluates performance across a limited set of representative materials, such as mild steel, stainless steel, aluminum alloys, and common engineering plastics (e.g.,",
